The open-set lattice of $X$ has a countable basis closed under finite unions such that every open $U$ is the union of basis opens $V\Subset U$. If $V\Subset U$, there is a compactly Cuntz supported $b_{V,U}\in Q(U)_+$ with $$Q(V)\subseteq\mathop{\mathrm{Ideal}}_Q(b_{V,U}).$$ Every compactly Cuntz supported $c\in Q(V)_+$ then satisfies $$\label{st:controller-multiple}
 [c]\leq N[b_{V,U}]
 \quad\hbox{for some finite }N\in\mathbb N.$$ The element $b_{V,U}$ is independent of $c$; the integer $N$ need not be.

Let $\omega$ be a free ultrafilter on $\mathbb N$, and let $\tau_n\in T(Q)$ be arbitrary. For a bounded positive sequence $(x_n)$, the formula $$\label{seq:regularization-formula}
 \rho([(x_n)])=
 \sup_{\varepsilon>0}\lim_{n\to\omega}\tau_n((x_n-\varepsilon)_+)$$ defines a lower-semicontinuous tracial weight on $D$. Its rank functional is $$\label{seq:regularized-rank}
 d_\rho([(x_n)])=
 \sup_{\varepsilon>0}\lim_{n\to\omega}d_{\tau_n}((x_n-\varepsilon)_+).$$ If $\sigma=\lim_{n\to\omega}\tau_n$ in the canonical topology on $T(Q)$, then $\rho|_Q=\sigma$.

Let $x,y\in D_+$. If there is $0<\alpha<1$ such that $$\label{seq:strict-rank-hypothesis}
 d_\rho(x)\leq\alpha d_\rho(y)
 \quad\hbox{for every regularized limit }\rho,$$ then $x\precsim y$. The same assertion holds after finite matrix amplification and after coordinatewise folding.

Let $p:S_Y\to D$ be a model, let $s=f\otimes a\geq0$, and suppose that $0<m_0:=m(f>0)<\infty$. For $r>0$, choose $c_r\in Q_+$ representing $rG[a]$. Then $$\label{seq:uncut-sandwich}
 c_r\precsim p(s)\quad(0<r<m_0),
 \qquad
 p(s)\precsim c_r\quad(r>m_0).$$ The following versions have fixed target cuts.*



*If $\eta,\delta>0$, $0<r<m(f>\eta)$, and $c$ represents $rG[(a-\delta)_+]$, then, for every $0<\kappa<\eta\delta$, $$\label{seq:lower-trimmed-sandwich}
 c\precsim(p(f\otimes a)-\kappa)_+.$$ If $r'>m_0$ and $\varepsilon>0$, then a representative $b$ of $r'G[a]$ has a positive cut $(b-\kappa)_+$ such that $$\label{seq:upper-trimmed-sandwich}
 (p(f\otimes a)-\varepsilon)_+\precsim(b-\kappa)_+.$$ The choices in these two displays can be made independently of the model $p$. The complete collection of such comparisons on the countable compact tests characterizes the model equation.

For a model $p$ and every $s\in S$, $$\label{seq:constant-ideal}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Ideal}}_D(p(s))=\mathop{\mathrm{Ideal}}_D(Q(U_s)).$$ Given countably many models $p_i:S\to D$, there is a separable subalgebra $D_0\subseteq D$ containing their ranges and the constants $Q$, such that, for every $\gamma\in T(D_0)$, $$\label{seq:all-trace-model-equation}
 \gamma\circ p_i=m\otimes F(\gamma|_Q)
 \quad\hbox{for every }i.$$ It may also contain any prescribed countable subset of $D$.

The definition of $J(U)$ is independent of representatives and makes it a closed two-sided ideal of $D$. The assignment is order preserving, and $$\label{seq:smallideal-support}
 J(U)\subseteq\mathop{\mathrm{Ideal}}_D(Q(U)).$$ If $\rho$ is a regularized limit and its restriction $\sigma=\rho|_Q$ is densely finite on $Q(U)$, then $$\label{seq:smallideal-vanishing}
 \rho|_{J(U)}=0.$$ All assertions use the same conventions in finite matrix algebras.

If $p,q:S\to D$ are models with the same data, then rational diagonal averaging, coordinate multiplier conjugacy, and the foldings of [st:folding](#st:folding) give models $\widetilde p,\widetilde q:S\to D$ with $$\label{un:local-difference}
 (\widetilde p-\widetilde q)(S(U))\subseteq J(U)
 \qquad(U\subseteq X\text{ open}).$$ Each new model is coordinate approximately unitarily equivalent to its respective original model. The operations may be performed in one changing block while a prescribed spare block is carried along unchanged, apart from its common amplification and folding.

There is a completely positive contraction $h:S\to D=Q_\infty$ with the following properties.*




*For every open $U\subseteq X$, $s\in S(U)$, and $z\in S$, $$\label{tc:mixed}
 h(sz)-h(s)h(z),\quad h(zs)-h(z)h(s)\ \in J(U).$$ If $s=f\otimes a\in\mathcal E$ is controlled inside $U$, and $\rho$ is a regularized limit trace whose restriction $\sigma=\rho|_Q$ is densely finite on $Q(U)$, then $$\label{tc:square}
 \rho(h(s)^2)=\left(\int_Y f^2\,dm\right)F(\sigma)(a^2).$$ Finally, for every $f\otimes a\in\mathcal E$ with $f\ne0$, $$\label{tc:lower-ideal}
 Q(U_a)\subseteq\mathop{\mathrm{Ideal}}_D\bigl(h(f\otimes a)\bigr).$$ The test family may be enlarged in advance by any countable family of the controlled elementary tests just described.

Fix finitely many controlled tests $s_i=f_i\otimes a_i$ and finitely many traces $\tau$ for each $i$, each satisfying $d_\tau(b_i)<\infty$. Take a separably acting representation $\pi$ containing the induced trace representations of their densely finite ideals, as well as any prescribed finite family of representations for bounded functionals on $Q$. The homomorphism $l$ in [tc:measurable-realization](#tc:measurable-realization) may be chosen so that, for the induced normal trace $\widetilde\tau$ on $M=\pi(Q)''$, $$\label{tc:finite-values}
 \widetilde\tau(l(s_i^k))
   =\left(\int f_i^k\,dm\right)F(\tau)(a_i^k),\qquad k=1,2.$$ This assertion requires no commutation of $F$ with a direct integral of extended weights.

Let $h:S\to D$ be c.p.c. and suppose that, for every open $U\subset X$, $$\label{cc:mixed-defects}
 h(sz)-h(s)h(z),\quad h(zs)-h(z)h(s)\in J(U)
 \qquad(s\in S(U),\ z\in S).$$ Let $E=C^*(h(S))$ and let $I\lhd E$ be the ideal generated by $h(ab)-h(a)h(b)$, for $a,b\in S$. Then $$\label{cc:annihilation}
 h(S(U))I+Ih(S(U))\subset J(U).$$ There is a locally small cone homomorphism $\theta:S\to D$ such that, after placing $h$ and $\theta$ in separate matrix corners, a $\sigma$-unital hereditary algebra $H\subset M_2(D)$ has the following properties. The first-corner map $h$ idealizes $H$, its multiplicative defects belong to $H$, and $\theta(S)\subset H$. Moreover, for every $x\in S_+$, $$\label{cc:strong-support}
 h(S(U_x))H+Hh(S(U_x))\subset H_x,
 \qquad H_x:=\mathop{\mathrm{Ideal}}_H(\theta(x)).$$ Here $\theta(S(U))\subset J(U)$, with the matrix conventions of [st:folding](#st:folding).

Let $d:S\to\mathcal L_H(H)=M(H)$ be the multiplier map induced by $h$ in [cc:defect-support](#cc:defect-support). There is a countably generated Hilbert $H$-module $\mathcal E'$ and a representation $$\label{cc:dilation}
 \Pi:S\longrightarrow\mathcal L_H(H\oplus\mathcal E'),
 \qquad
 \Pi(a)=
 \begin{pmatrix}
 d(a)&C(a)\\ B(a)&d'(a)
 \end{pmatrix},$$ whose off-diagonal entries are compact. The maps $d,d'$ are c.p.c. The module may be stabilized, retaining the first copy of $H$, so that $\mathcal E'$ is the standard countable module.*


*Give $S$ its tight $\mathop{\mathrm{Prim}}(S)$-labels and give $H$ the order-preserving assignment $$\label{cc:fine-labels}
 H(O)=\mathop{\mathrm{Ideal}}_H(\theta(S(O)))
 \qquad(O\subset\mathop{\mathrm{Prim}}(S)\text{ open}).$$ Let $\alpha_t$ be cone scaling, $$(\alpha_t a)(r)=a(tr),\qquad 0\le t\le1,$$ where $a(0)=0$. Every $\Pi_t:=\Pi\alpha_t$ is weakly $\mathop{\mathrm{Prim}}(S)$-nuclear for <a href="#cc:fine-labels" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="cc:fine-labels">[cc:fine-labels]</a>. The same is true of its diagonal compressions $d_t,d'_t$.

Under the hypotheses of [cc:defect-support](#cc:defect-support), let $\mathcal F$ be a finite subset of the unit ball of $S$ and let $\eta>0$. There are an integer $N$, a bounded linear $*$-preserving map $L:S\to M_{N+1}(M_2(D))$, and a bounded linear map $K_N:S\to M_{N+1}(H)$ such that $$\label{cc:finite-form}
 L(a)=h(a)\oplus\theta(a)^{\oplus N}+K_N(a),$$ and, for $a,b\in\mathcal F$, $$\label{cc:finite-estimates}
 \|L(a)\|\le\|a\|+\eta,
 \qquad
 \|L(ab)-L(a)L(b)\|\le3\eta+2\eta^2.$$ For every open $U\subset X$, $$\label{cc:finite-locality}
 K_N(S(U))\subset M_{N+1}(H\cap M_2(J(U))).$$ The first summand in <a href="#cc:finite-form" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="cc:finite-form">[cc:finite-form]</a> is the original $h$, with only zero corner padding.

Suppose $h:S\to D$ is c.p.c. and satisfies <a href="#cc:mixed-defects" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="cc:mixed-defects">[cc:mixed-defects]</a>. After trace-preserving coordinate corner padding, there is a homomorphism $\lambda:S\to D$ such that $$\label{cc:corrected-difference}
 (\lambda-h^{\mathrm{pad}})(S(U))\subset J(U)
 \qquad(U\subset X\text{ open}).$$ The padding can be chosen to fix the constant copy of $Q$ in the sequence quotient. Consequently it preserves the constant ideal comparisons and the regularized trace formulas prescribed for $h$.

There is a model $$\lambda:C_0(\mathbb R)\otimes P\longrightarrow D$$ for Lebesgue measure and $F$. Let $\alpha(f)(t)=f(t-1)$, acting trivially on $P$. For c.p.c. coordinate lifts $\lambda_n$, there are unitaries $u_n\in M(Q)$ such that $$\label{pt:coordinate-covariance}
 \|\mathop{\mathrm{Ad}}(u_n)(\lambda_n(s))-\lambda_n(\alpha(s))\|
 \longrightarrow0\qquad(s\in C_0(\mathbb R)\otimes P).$$

There are $\zeta\in C_c(\mathbb R)_+$ and a projection $p\in E$ such that $$\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}\zeta(t-n)^2=1,
 \qquad \int_\mathbb R\zeta(t)^2\,dt=1,$$ and, for any $C^*$-algebra $R_0$, any extended trace $\omega$ on $E\otimes R_0$, and every $a\in(R_0)_+$, $$\label{pt:corner-trace}
 \omega(p\otimes a)=\omega(\zeta^2\otimes a).$$ In particular, if the restriction of $\omega$ to $C_0(\mathbb R)\otimes R_0$ is Lebesgue measure tensored with $\nu$, then $\omega(p\otimes a)=\nu(a)$, including the value infinity. Moreover, $p$ is full and $pEp\cong C(\mathbb R/\mathbb Z)$.
